WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.159 --> 00:00:08.910
About 130 million years ago during the early
Cretaceous period, something rather unexpected

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:09.910
happened.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:11.230
A flower bloomed.

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:15.940
This was undoubtedly awkward for the plant
involved.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:17.800
Nobody had ever seen a flower before.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:24.710
The plants all around it were mosses and seed-bearing
pines, cycads and ferns.

00:00:24.710 --> 00:00:29.130
They probably called the floral shrub names
and made jokes about the length of its stamen.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.259
You know how cruel plants can be.

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:37.010
Exactly why and how this happened is not entirely
clear.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:42.970
Looking totally different from any other vegetation
of the time and with no obvious evolutionary

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:49.820
ancestors, the emergence of flowering plants
remains a botanical conundrum to this day.

00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:55.050
In 1879, a grumpy Charles Darwin called it
an “abominable mystery.”

00:00:55.050 --> 00:01:01.489
What bugged him was that flowering plants,
collectively known as angiosperms, quite inconveniently

00:01:01.489 --> 00:01:05.970
didn’t follow his theory of gradual evolution.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:11.970
Like hip hop artists in the 1970s, they appeared
to have arrived from nowhere and then grown

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:15.080
quickly in volume and diversity.

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:20.530
In just a hundred million years, flowering
plants have conquered almost every habitat

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:26.530
on earth, today accounting for 9 out of every
10 plant species on the planet.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:33.440
They have redefined the natural landscape
and formed the foundation of new ecosystems.

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:38.000
And without them, we’d all be on the Atkins
diet.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.560
Recently published research suggests Darwin
was fretting over nothing: angiosperms quite

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:49.380
possibly evolved progressively and over a
suitably long period of time, though their

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:55.410
relative fragility makes finding them in the
fossil record harder than it is compared to,

00:01:55.410 --> 00:02:00.630
say, dinosaurs, who let’s face it could
have stood to lose a few pounds.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:07.649
Still, amgiosperms’ eventual total dominance
of the plant kingdom remains one of nature’s

00:02:07.649 --> 00:02:09.250
great success stories.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:14.070
But let’s not hype flowers too much - they
couldn’t have achieved this triumph on their

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:15.070
own.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:19.670
All the angiosperm in the world is useless
to you if you can’t even stroll over to

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:22.990
another plant and buy her a drink.

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:30.640
No, flowering plants need help to reproduce,
and no-one has facilitated more plant-on-plant

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:33.730
action than the humble bee.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:40.460
Fossilised nests of bee or wasp-like creatures
found in the Petrified Forest of Arizona date

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:47.610
back around 220 million years, before flowers
existed in the form we know today.

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:53.080
But scientists think that, back then, there
probably wasn’t much of a buzz between the

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.490
blossoms and the bees.

00:02:55.490 --> 00:02:58.800
Flowers were pretty ugly and bees only went
for cheerleaders.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:04.319
But at some stage plants appear to have realised
they were missing a trick.

00:03:04.319 --> 00:03:09.990
Flowers began to evolve to be more attractive
to flying insects, especially bees.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:16.470
90 to 100 million years ago they developed
petals and started competing with each other

00:03:16.470 --> 00:03:23.090
to lure the most pollinators, a process believed
to be partially responsible for their rapid

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:26.950
diversification and colonisation of the earth.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:33.130
In response, bees changed their shape, structure,
and behaviour to optimise their access to

00:03:33.130 --> 00:03:34.530
pollen and nectar.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:41.390
It’s a famous story of co-evolution that
has seen flowers and bees live in mutually-beneficial

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:44.140
harmony for millions of years.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.390
Until recently, that is.

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:52.750
Because the world’s bees are dying, and
nobody knows exactly why.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:04:00.310
In mid-November 2006, American beekeeper Dave
Hackenberg made a discovery that would shake

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.349
the beekeeping world.

00:04:02.349 --> 00:04:10.319
In a routine inspection of his 2400 hives,
he found 400 of them totally abandoned.

00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:12.349
The bees were gone.

00:04:12.349 --> 00:04:14.390
Not dead, gone.

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:16.540
Like little buzzy ghosts.

00:04:16.540 --> 00:04:22.310
By February 2007, several large beekeeping
operations in the US had reported similar

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:27.310
cases, with some losing as many as 90% of
their colonies.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:32.870
Similar disappearances had happened throughout
the history of beekeeping, also known as apiculture,

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:36.840
with the first recorded case dating back to
1869.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:41.170
The phenomenon has been called by many names
over the years, including “disappearing

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:45.160
disease”, “May disease” or “Spring
Dwindle”.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:51.000
But what took place in 2006 was unprecedented
in its scale.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:58.160
So, they gave it a new name: Colony Collapse
Disorder, or CCD.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:05.100
The original name was going to be Colony Upheaval
and neutralisation Tragedies, but then someone

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.470
pointed out that the acronym was rather problematic.

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:14.740
What’s really strange about CUN - I mean
CCD is that there weren’t any dead bees

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:18.100
left behind, just empty hives.

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:23.350
It’s as if one morning a lone worker bee
said, “Screw this, we work too hard.

00:05:23.350 --> 00:05:25.710
I’m going to Mexico.”

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:31.020
And after some furious waggle-dancing 80 000
bees followed him.

00:05:31.020 --> 00:05:36.270
Some newspapers started referring to it as
the Bee Rapture, while in the UK they called

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:42.560
it Mary Celeste syndrome after the merchant
ship discovered off the Azores in 1872 with

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:46.710
not a single passenger aboard - I’ll be
covering that story in an upcoming video,

00:05:46.710 --> 00:05:49.500
by the way – subscribe so you don’t miss
it.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:55.340
For approximately the next nine years, CCD
claimed about a third of all bee colonies

00:05:55.340 --> 00:06:03.460
in the United States every year, without anyone
being able to work out exactly why.

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:05.560
And then it just… stopped.

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:11.350
We’re not really sure why that happened
either - do we actually know anything? - but

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:16.131
for about the last five years we haven’t
seen that particular set of issues, or at

00:06:16.131 --> 00:06:19.050
least not to anywhere near the same extent.

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:21.310
Good news, right?

00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:23.680
Unfortunately not.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:30.670
Bee populations have continued to nosedive,
with more than 30% of tracked American honey

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:38.260
bee colonies dying off every year for the
last decade or so, up to around 40% last year

00:06:38.260 --> 00:06:39.310
and the year before.

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:44.740
But it’s not only the US who are experiencing
this - it’s a global trend.

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:50.800
Hanyuan county in China's Sichuan province
is known as the pear capital of the world,

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:56.460
but with local bee numbers in steep decline,
the annual pollination needed to ensure a

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:03.639
full crop of fruit is now done mostly by hand,
one flower at a time.

00:07:03.639 --> 00:07:10.259
And in Germany, a decades-long study of protected
nature reserves found that 75% of flying insects

00:07:10.259 --> 00:07:15.110
in these areas have died over the last 30
years or so.

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:20.270
Some scientists suggest that this is all part
of a wider mass extinction event in which

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:24.040
the majority of the planet’s species will
die off.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:31.150
This has happened five times before that we’re
aware of, and we could be witnessing the 6th

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:32.150
right now.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:37.940
After all, populations of animals all over
the planet are declining so rapidly that researchers

00:07:37.940 --> 00:07:42.180
say we’re in a process of "biological annihilation."

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:47.890
That might seem hard to believe, but only
because when people say “endangered animal”

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:52.620
you probably automatically think of some of
the big ticket beasties like tigers and rhinos..

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:58.720
But insects make up around 90% of all species
of animals on the planet, and more than half

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:00.560
of all living things.

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:08.080
There are approximately 1.4 billion insects
for every human on earth, but around the world

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:11.449
that ratio is rapidly declining.

00:08:11.449 --> 00:08:14.770
The obvious question is, “Why should you
care?”

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:17.770
Nobody likes insects anyway, right?

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:21.789
Especially ones like bees that can fight back
when you piss them off.

00:08:21.789 --> 00:08:26.970
But insects, and particularly the bee, are
crucial to human survival.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:34.229
Besides the honey bee, there are actually
25 to 30 000 different bee species, and they

00:08:34.229 --> 00:08:36.070
could all be suffering too.

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:42.679
That matters because without them we wouldn’t
have most of the food we eat on a daily basis.

00:08:42.679 --> 00:08:45.290
Remember the flowering plants I mentioned
earlier?

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:51.980
They include the fruit, vegetables and crops
that make up the bulk of our natural diet,

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:56.629
and they’re all pollinated by bees and other
insects.

00:08:56.629 --> 00:09:04.639
According to the UN, up to 75% of world food
crops depend on bees and other pollinators

00:09:04.639 --> 00:09:10.249
in some way, while one international study
estimates the annual global food production

00:09:10.249 --> 00:09:15.089
that depends on pollinators is worth half
a trillion dollars.

00:09:15.089 --> 00:09:21.319
Without insects, but especially bees, our
food system wouldn’t necessarily collapse,

00:09:21.319 --> 00:09:25.019
but many more people would go hungry.

00:09:25.019 --> 00:09:29.439
We would still be able to grow staples like
grain and cereals which are pollinated by

00:09:29.439 --> 00:09:35.959
the wind, but fruits and vegetables would
be unavailable or unaffordable At a minimum

00:09:35.959 --> 00:09:40.990
you would lose access to good nutrition and
most of the stuff that you like to eat.

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:45.209
Unless you’re super rich you’d probably
have to start familiarising yourself with

00:09:45.209 --> 00:09:49.740
the symptoms of scurvy, and you likely wouldn’t
be able to get your hands on coffee.

00:09:49.740 --> 00:09:53.269
I’ll let that one sink in for a moment.

00:09:53.269 --> 00:09:54.269
No.

00:09:54.269 --> 00:09:55.269
Coffee.

00:09:55.269 --> 00:10:00.189
Beyond our human concerns, if all the bees
died off the ripple effects would be felt

00:10:00.189 --> 00:10:03.670
throughout most major natural ecosystems.

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:09.089
Without pollinators there would be fewer plants,
which would ultimately mean less food for

00:10:09.089 --> 00:10:14.149
most of earth’s animal species and more
extinctions of all kinds.

00:10:14.149 --> 00:10:17.160
Basically, an armageddon of the natural world.

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:21.800
It’s a pretty bleak picture, so how do we
stop it from happening?

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:25.019
First step, of course, is to understand the
problem.

00:10:25.019 --> 00:10:29.519
Easier said than done, since this is a complex
issue with many moving parts.

00:10:29.519 --> 00:10:35.040
And no, it’s not, as some have suggested,
caused by cellphones, electromagnetic radiation,

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:41.540
a Russian mind control experiment, or aliens
- aliens really do get blamed for a lot of

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:42.699
stuff don’t they?

00:10:42.699 --> 00:10:44.800
It’s no wonder they never visit.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:49.759
But scientists have identified a number of
things that could be impacting the populations

00:10:49.759 --> 00:10:52.170
of all insect pollinators.

00:10:52.170 --> 00:10:57.679
Perhaps the most obvious candidate is climate
change, which impacts all ecosystems, but

00:10:57.679 --> 00:11:04.250
there isn’t yet much evidence to directly
link climate change to insect die-off.

00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:06.709
Another concern is habitat destruction.

00:11:06.709 --> 00:11:11.569
Humans have a nasty tendency of taking over
natural environments to build things like

00:11:11.569 --> 00:11:18.130
car parks, shopping malls, and starbucks outlets,
leaving less room for other species to live

00:11:18.130 --> 00:11:19.389
and grow.

00:11:19.389 --> 00:11:25.480
Strongly related to this is modern agriculture,
which sees increasing areas of land, usually

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:29.820
forests, being cleared and repurposed to grow
food.

00:11:29.820 --> 00:11:34.399
In the last 50 years, the human population
has nearly doubled while the average calories

00:11:34.399 --> 00:11:39.540
consumed per person has increased by about
30%, so the theory goes that we need more

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:41.339
land to grow more food.

00:11:41.339 --> 00:11:45.490
There’s a lot of debate about that particular
point, not least because a lot of the food

00:11:45.490 --> 00:11:47.550
we grow is being fed to animals, not people.

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:51.489
About a third of all arable land, in fact,
is used to grow crops that feed livestock.

00:11:51.489 --> 00:11:57.589
But we’ll save that debate for another time.

00:11:57.589 --> 00:12:02.240
Climate change and habitat destruction can
partly account for declining populations of

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:07.860
wild animals, but they don’t sufficiently
explain why so many commercial and domestic

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:11.549
honey bees are dying too.

00:12:11.549 --> 00:12:18.589
To understand that we need to consider the
three Ps: pesticides, poor nutrition, and

00:12:18.589 --> 00:12:20.029
parasites.

00:12:20.029 --> 00:12:25.799
To increase size and reliability of yields,
commercial farming relies heavily on the use

00:12:25.799 --> 00:12:28.559
of pesticides and insecticides.

00:12:28.559 --> 00:12:35.319
It stands to reason that bees, being insects,
do not react particularly well to these substances,

00:12:35.319 --> 00:12:37.800
but some are worse than others.

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:44.939
A relatively new class of insecticides, neonicotinoids,
are used widely in agriculture today and may

00:12:44.939 --> 00:12:50.989
be outright deadly to bees, short-circuiting
their memory and navigation.

00:12:50.989 --> 00:12:56.970
As the name implies, these chemicals have
an unintentional nicotine-like addicting effect

00:12:56.970 --> 00:13:01.470
on the bees, causing them to repeatedly come
back for more.

00:13:01.470 --> 00:13:07.329
Other pesticides have less lethal effects,
but they work to weaken bees’ immune systems,

00:13:07.329 --> 00:13:11.319
making them more susceptible to disease and
parasites.

00:13:11.319 --> 00:13:15.100
Making matters worse is a mass drop in the
quality of bee nutrition.

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:19.709
Left to its own devices, your average honey
bee would spend its day humming show tunes

00:13:19.709 --> 00:13:24.299
and flitting from flower to flower, drinking
nectar and collecting pollen.

00:13:24.299 --> 00:13:29.050
Then it would fly home to its hive, where
the nectar would be converted into honey,

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:34.199
and the pollen would be used as a protein
food source in the form of bee bread.

00:13:34.199 --> 00:13:38.629
In this scenario, a bee might visit hundreds
of different flowers from different plants

00:13:38.629 --> 00:13:42.809
every day, enjoying a diverse diet in the
process.

00:13:42.809 --> 00:13:48.689
However, modern farming methods tend to favour
monoculture, the practice of planting massive

00:13:48.689 --> 00:13:51.829
areas of land with a single type of plant.

00:13:51.829 --> 00:13:58.060
At its worst, this might be thousands of hectares
of farm land planted with commercial crops

00:13:58.060 --> 00:14:02.699
like soy or corn, which aren’t pollinated
by insects.

00:14:02.699 --> 00:14:06.259
To a bee that’s the equivalent of an empty
supermarket.

00:14:06.259 --> 00:14:11.199
A food desert that will effectively lead to
starvation.

00:14:11.199 --> 00:14:15.049
Monocultures of flowering crops aren’t much
better, though.

00:14:15.049 --> 00:14:21.519
California produces 80% of the world’s almonds,
with more than 1.4 million acres of land dedicated

00:14:21.519 --> 00:14:25.350
to almond trees and very little else.

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:30.439
Come spring, that’s a lot of almond flowers,
and those need a lot of bees.

00:14:30.439 --> 00:14:38.069
Every February, an incredible 85% of all commercial
bee colonies in America are taken to California

00:14:38.069 --> 00:14:43.110
for the single largest managed pollination
event anywhere in the world.

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:49.160
The demand is so great that even more hives
are flown in from countries like Australia.

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:54.360
On the surface it seems like bee paradise:
hundreds of miles of almond flowers - what

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:56.339
more could a bee want?.

00:14:56.339 --> 00:15:00.899
But because all the blossoms are from one
variety of plant, the bee’s diet becomes

00:15:00.899 --> 00:15:05.260
one-dimensional, lacking sufficient nutritional
diversity.

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:08.959
Imagine if you only ate one thing all day,
every day.

00:15:08.959 --> 00:15:13.819
You wouldn’t starve, but you wouldn’t
be very healthy, either - even if that thing

00:15:13.819 --> 00:15:15.199
was broccoli.

00:15:15.199 --> 00:15:21.310
As a result, bee immune systems become compromised,
an effect made worse by the large quantity

00:15:21.310 --> 00:15:24.019
of pesticides used in the almond groves.

00:15:24.019 --> 00:15:30.399
So, with pesticides and poor nutrition creating
weaker bees, the stage is set for the final

00:15:30.399 --> 00:15:36.619
P in our puzzle, parasites, and one vicious
bastard in particular: the varroa destructor

00:15:36.619 --> 00:15:37.619
mite.

00:15:37.619 --> 00:15:43.429
This horror story of a creature is only about
1.2 millimetres long, but it’s capable of

00:15:43.429 --> 00:15:45.670
doing some serious damage.

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:51.049
It starts by climbing onto the back of a bee,
where it proceeds to wedge itself between

00:15:51.049 --> 00:15:54.129
the plates of the bee’s exoskeleton.

00:15:54.129 --> 00:16:01.499
Once there, it pierces the skin, injecting
a cocktail of digestive enzymes that gradually

00:16:01.499 --> 00:16:07.559
liquify the bee’s insides, providing the
mite with an all-you-can-eat bee-smoothie

00:16:07.559 --> 00:16:08.949
buffet.

00:16:08.949 --> 00:16:10.339
Delicious.

00:16:10.339 --> 00:16:15.079
Hives used to be able to withstand varroa
attacks, provided the infestation didn’t

00:16:15.079 --> 00:16:18.419
overwhelm more than 20% of the colony.

00:16:18.419 --> 00:16:23.859
But nowadays, with weakened immune systems
and pesticide-resistant strains of varroa

00:16:23.859 --> 00:16:30.059
mite, the survival threshold is only about
3% of the bee population.

00:16:30.059 --> 00:16:33.209
After that, the hive is decimated.

00:16:33.209 --> 00:16:37.929
And it's been happening more and more for
the past three decades.

00:16:37.929 --> 00:16:43.959
Solving the bee genocide issue isn’t rocket
science, which is lucky because most of the

00:16:43.959 --> 00:16:46.139
scientists working on the problem are entomologists.

00:16:46.139 --> 00:16:51.699
But it is a problem that needs to be addressed
if your kids hope to be eating something other

00:16:51.699 --> 00:17:05.470
than gruel in 50 years’ time.

